WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.149
Human review only adds safety when the pause lands in the right place and the handoff tells the

2
00:00:05.149 --> 00:00:07.425
reviewer what they're actually looking at.

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:13.680
For this video, we'll look at where in the workflow to interrupt, what a good handoff message contains,

4
00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:22.200
how to preserve state so the agent can resume after approval, and how async escalations work. Let's jump into it.

5
00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:29.979
A pause is only as good as the design behind it. An interruption point is a defined location in an agentic workflow.

6
00:00:30.079 --> 00:00:35.490
Where the agent stops and waits for human input and its placement decides whether that pause

7
00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:36.961
actually buys any safety.

8
00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:44.439
State management is the set of mechanisms that persist task progress and agent decisions across workflow steps.

9
00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:50.778
The constraint that trips people up is that in-context state is lost when the context window is

10
00:00:50.778 --> 00:00:52.575
truncated or a session ends.

11
00:00:52.639 --> 00:00:59.360
So anything that matters has to be stored externally. And a fallback chain is the ordered sequence of alternatives.

12
00:00:59.439 --> 00:01:05.245
The agent tries when a primary action fails. Each one has to be pre-validated so it doesn't

13
00:01:05.245 --> 00:01:07.096
introduce a new failure mode.

14
00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:13.636
Where you place an interruption point is one of the highest leveraged decisions in HITL design.

15
00:01:13.636 --> 00:01:15.942
Three placements are consistently valid.

16
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:22.599
Before irreversible actions is the most critical because that's where a pause prevents an uncorrectable error,

17
00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:28.415
which is the whole point of human review. At phase boundaries makes sense because these are natural

18
00:01:28.415 --> 00:01:31.982
transition points where the agent's scope or authority shifts.

19
00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:38.199
Asking a human to confirm that the next phase should proceed is reasonable and low overhead.

20
00:01:38.959 --> 00:01:45.800
Anomaly detection covers unexpected inputs, unusual patterns, or values outside the normal operating range.

21
00:01:46.199 --> 00:01:51.480
One placement that provides no meaningful safety benefit is after irreversible actions.

22
00:01:52.199 --> 00:01:57.578
Reviewing what already happened doesn't prevent the error. Placement before the action is what makes

23
00:01:57.578 --> 00:01:58.546
the review useful.

24
00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:06.599
A handoff message that dumps raw tool output at a reviewer forces them to do the agent's analytical work themselves.

25
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:12.300
That defeats the purpose. A well-designed handoff message answers three questions immediately.

26
00:02:12.759 --> 00:02:15.160
What was the agent doing and why did it pause?

27
00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:21.179
Give the reviewer enough context to orient quickly without jargon or internal model terminology.

28
00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:27.639
What decision is required? This should be a specific answerable question, not a vague request for review.

29
00:02:28.119 --> 00:02:33.160
And what are the consequences? What follows from approval and what follows from rejection?

30
00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:38.460
Reviewers who understand the downstream impact make faster and better decisions.

31
00:02:38.919 --> 00:02:44.252
A handoff message that does this translation work turns human review from a bottleneck into a

32
00:02:44.252 --> 00:02:45.742
genuine safety checkpoint.

33
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.500
A good handoff message has four components.

34
00:02:50.119 --> 00:02:53.580
Context is what the agent was doing and why it paused.

35
00:02:54.059 --> 00:02:58.220
Written in plain language without internal jargon or raw tool output.

36
00:02:58.779 --> 00:03:03.899
Decision required is the exact thing the reviewer is being asked to approve or reject.

37
00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:08.539
Stated as a specific question rather than an open-ended review request.

38
00:03:09.399 --> 00:03:12.539
Consequences are what happens downstream from each choice.

39
00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:18.508
So the reviewer understands what they're authorizing and the deadline says when this review request

40
00:03:18.508 --> 00:03:20.562
expires and what happens at timeout.

41
00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:27.039
Omitting the deadline creates ambiguity about urgency and leaves the agent design incomplete.

42
00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:33.419
Reviewers should always know how long they have and what the fallback is if they don't respond in time.

43
00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:38.619
These four components give reviewers everything they need to make a real decision quickly.

44
00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:44.139
State preservation is what determines whether a paused workflow is resumable or disposable.

45
00:03:44.699 --> 00:03:50.940
If the agent stores nothing before pausing, the only option when approval arrives is to restart from scratch.

46
00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:54.779
That wastes time and may produce different outputs on retry.

47
00:03:55.339 --> 00:04:01.339
What needs to be persisted? Task progress, meaning what steps completed and what outputs were produced.

48
00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:06.619
And agent decisions, meaning what the model chose at each branch point.

49
00:04:06.619 --> 00:04:10.940
The critical constraint is that you can't rely on in-context state for this.

50
00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:17.251
The context window can be truncated and the session can end between when the agent pauses and when

51
00:04:17.251 --> 00:04:18.475
the human responds.

52
00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:23.739
External persistence in a database or structured store is the only reliable mechanism.

53
00:04:24.299 --> 00:04:29.899
State preservation separates a workflow that can pick up where it left off from one that has to start over.

54
00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:35.339
Synchronous and async escalation handle the blocking question differently.

55
00:04:35.579 --> 00:04:43.339
Synchronous escalation means the agent halts completely and waits for human approval before doing anything else.

56
00:04:43.339 --> 00:04:48.700
It's simple to implement, but the entire workflow is blocked during the review period.

57
00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:53.899
That's appropriate when the dependent work genuinely can't proceed without the decision.

58
00:04:53.899 --> 00:04:59.916
Async escalation means the agent queues the review request and continues working on independent

59
00:04:59.916 --> 00:05:01.436
branches while it waits.

60
00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:08.004
Non-blocking, but it requires more state management because now you have work proceeding in parallel

61
00:05:08.004 --> 00:05:09.434
with a pending review.

62
00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:15.500
Async escalation is appropriate when independent work can move forward without the pending decision.

63
00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:21.515
The choice between the two comes down to whether anything else in the workflow actually needs the

64
00:05:21.515 --> 00:05:23.437
decision before it can proceed.

65
00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:27.500
Resumption design is the other half of state preservation.

66
00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:33.500
When approval arrives, the agent needs enough context to continue without rerunning completed work.

67
00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:37.500
The approval message should include the decision and any notes the reviewer added.

68
00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:41.500
The agent then loads persisted state and resumes from the interruption point.

69
00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:43.500
There are a couple of traps here.

70
00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:49.500
Rerunning completed steps wastes time and can produce different outputs if the underlying data has changed.

71
00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:53.500
And don't assume the agent can reconstruct context on its own.

72
00:05:53.500 --> 00:05:57.500
After an interruption, the agent's active context may be gone.

73
00:05:57.500 --> 00:06:01.500
The persisted state is the only source of truth for what happened before the pause.

74
00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:07.500
Design the resumption path explicitly as part of the workflow design, not as an afterthought.

75
00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:11.500
Async escalation requires explicit design for the timeout case.

76
00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:19.500
When you queue a review and continue working, you need to define what happens if no human responds before the pause.

77
00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:21.500
Three components matter.

78
00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:27.500
The queue and continue pattern has the agent proceed on independent branches while the review is pending,

79
00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:31.500
requiring careful state tracking to know what's waiting.

80
00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:35.500
The timeout threshold is a specific time limit after which a fallback is triggered.

81
00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:41.500
And the timeout action is to escalate the request further, abort the task entirely,

82
00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:45.500
or apply a predefined safe default and continue.

83
00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:47.500
What you can't do is silently hang.

84
00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:49.500
An unhandled timeout is a silent failure.

85
00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:55.500
The workflow appears to be running, but is actually stuck waiting for a response that may never come.

86
00:06:55.500 --> 00:07:01.500
Define the timeout threshold and the timeout action before deploying any async escalation pattern.

87
00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:07.500
Think of human review as a gate that opens up a path for the agent to proceed.

88
00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:13.500
Think of human review as a gate that only works when both parts land.

89
00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:18.500
There's the moment the agent stops, and there's the message that reaches the person standing there.

90
00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:21.500
A pause in the wrong place reviews nothing.

91
00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:25.500
A pause with a vague message gets rubber stamp approval.

92
00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:29.500
What holds the pattern together is state you can persist,

93
00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:34.500
because approval an hour later is worthless if the work restarts from scratch.

94
00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:38.500
That's it for this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

